WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
INTRO:
Recently, I was recommended a video called “How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:09.240
Much FAANG Pays Software Engineers In 2022”. Now,&nbsp;
we all know that FAANG software engineers make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:16.500
fat salary, so I went into the video expecting&nbsp;
$200 to $300,000. But, I was blown away by how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:22.620
much these guys actually make. Take Facebook for&nbsp;
example. An entry level software engineer themself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:30.240
nearly crosses $200,000 per year. With just 2&nbsp;
promotions this nearly doubles to $400,000 per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:36.240
year and we’re only half way through. If you&nbsp;
make it to level 7, you’d be making nearly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.160
million dollars per year, and if you make it all&nbsp;
the way to level 8 which is principal engineer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:48.000
you’d be making $1.6 million per year. A very&nbsp;
similar pay scale can be found at Google and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.160
other silicon valley companies as well. Now, let’s&nbsp;
be clear here, we’re not talking about VPs or C&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.780
suite executives. We’re talking about high ranking&nbsp;
coders like IT directors and they’re pulling in 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:06.240
figures. For perspective, there are literally&nbsp;
CEOs of everyday brands that make a comparable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:13.680
amount. DISH CEO Erik Carlson, for example, made&nbsp;
$2.9 million last year. And he’s literally the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.740
CEO. If we take a look at lower ranking C suite&nbsp;
executives, many of them actually make less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:26.220
these FAANG software engineers. Stuart A Levy&nbsp;
for example is the CFO of Domino’s pizza and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:33.360
“only” made $1.57 million last year. Also, we&nbsp;
should note that Domino’s pizza was literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.660
the second fastest growing stock of the 2010s,&nbsp;
only beaten out by Netflix. So, these executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:45.540
beat Facebook, Apple, Amazon, Google, and the&nbsp;
rest of the fortune 500 in terms of stock growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.000
for an entire decade. Yet, their compensation&nbsp;
is literally lower than principal engineers at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.460
FAANG. Either the executives at these second tier&nbsp;
companies are getting ripped off or FAANG is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:01.800
flexing. Historically, the most straight forward&nbsp;
way to become rich at a young age without starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:07.320
a business was to grind out your 20s at an&nbsp;
investment bank or hedgefund. But, it looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.300
FAANG has completely thrown that out the window&nbsp;
with their compensation packages. For a highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.340
ambitious individual who is recruited straight out&nbsp;
of college or even without college, 2 promotions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.080
at FAANG seems pretty reasonable within 10 years&nbsp;
meaning that they could be earning nearly $400,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:29.640
in the 30s. And here’s the cool thing, that’s a&nbsp;
2022 salary. By the time you actually spend 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:36.780
years in the industry, this salary would probably&nbsp;
be closer to $6500,000 per year if not more. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.600
if you only get one promotion after that, you’d&nbsp;
probably making close to 7 figures if not 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:48.300
figures. In other words, if you become a software&nbsp;
engineer today and make it into these top tier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.520
companies, you have a solid chance of making 7&nbsp;
figures by the time your in your 50s. Seeing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.400
it’s no wonder why so many people are choosing to&nbsp;
major in computer science or STEM in general. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.260
this begs the question, why are software&nbsp;
engineers paid so much in the first place?

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:08.820
CODING SHORTAGE:&nbsp;

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:14.460
This might be hard to believe, but there’s&nbsp;
actually a massive shortage when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:20.640
software developers. In fact, as of 2019, there&nbsp;
were 1 million unfulfilled IT jobs in the US.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:27.420
At first glance, this probably makes no sense.&nbsp;
Nowadays, there’s such a massive emphasis on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:32.340
understanding code even if you’re not pursuing&nbsp;
software engineering. I mean, even elementary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.440
schoolers are being taught code. And as you&nbsp;
go into middle school and high school, there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.600
dozens of classes for coding that are usually&nbsp;
filled to the brim. And as for college, if you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.760
getting any sort of STEM degree or even a business&nbsp;
degree at this point, it’s basically impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:54.420
to graduate without taking atleast 1 or 2 coding&nbsp;
classes. And all of that is just within academia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.420 --> 00:04:00.840
itself. Outside academia, we have hundreds of&nbsp;
bootcamps and courses on Skillshare and Udemy. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.880
to mention the tens of thousands of free videos&nbsp;
and articles available on YouTube and Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:12.060
Also, it’s not like the average person is ignorant&nbsp;
to coding either like they were back in the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.200
Virtually everyone in the world understands&nbsp;
how important computer science is to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:21.780
modern world and how lucrative a CS career could&nbsp;
be. Considering all this, you would think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.140
there’s an oversupply of developers, yet this is&nbsp;
not the case. So, what is creating this disparity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:33.840
between how many people learn coding and how many&nbsp;
people actually get a job in coding? Well, at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.580
root of it, there can only be two explanations&nbsp;
for this: it’s either the employer's fault or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:44.340
employees fault. Maybe the employers are being&nbsp;
too picky or expecting too much. But, when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:50.280
look at the data, this is simply not the case.&nbsp;
Most tech companies have actually been lowering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:55.140
their standards like dropping degree requirements.&nbsp;
Now, you can argue all you want that these tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.140 --> 00:05:00.840
companies still prefer college graduates, but&nbsp;
that’s besides the point. This video isn’t about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:06.480
whether college is worth. The point is that these&nbsp;
tech companies are objectively making it easier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.420
get a job with them. Some research from Indeed&nbsp;
further confirms this. 80% of US tech managers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:18.540
have selected candidates that graduated from a&nbsp;
coding boot camp program. And 99.8% of them are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.300
willing to hire similar candidates again. So,&nbsp;
if anything, employers are desperate and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:29.340
is extremely evident in their highly generous&nbsp;
compensation programs. But, if the shortage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:34.920
isn’t caused by employers, that means that it&nbsp;
must caused by employees, and that’s exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:40.800
what’s happening. The coding industry as a whole&nbsp;
has a massive churn problem. Developers come in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.680
they stay for a few years, and then they leave.&nbsp;
The average tenure at all of the top companies is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:53.880
literally at max 2 years. Now, of course, tenure&nbsp;
is skewed by new hires, but it’s not like turnover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:00.180
would be that much better anyway. I mean, high&nbsp;
turnover at these companies is not a secret. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.160
have a full video about this phenomenon if you’re&nbsp;
interested, but at the crux of it, more times than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.440
not, coders just get burnt out. This is simply&nbsp;
worsened by the fact that a substantial number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.600
of developers don’t like to code to begin with.&nbsp;
The only reason they pursued a CS job is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.640
their parents told them or because the career made&nbsp;
sense to them objectively from a money or prestige&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:26.340
perspective. But, when it comes to the job itself,&nbsp;
they’re not actually that excited. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.440
you just sit in front of a computer for 8 hours&nbsp;
a day and browse through stackoverflow. Even for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.460
the people who like coding, they usually don’t&nbsp;
like the coding they have to do at Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:41.160
and Google. It’s not like they’re building the&nbsp;
next Instagram or YouTube. More times than not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.480
they’re banging their heads on the wall trying&nbsp;
to refine a data sorting algorithm or fixing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:51.840
menial bugs. And after 40 years, they don’t&nbsp;
really have much to show for any of it. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.940
are they gonna say, I made the Facebook experience&nbsp;
indistinguishably better? Now, this isn’t to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:02.160
that all software engineering jobs are bad. The&nbsp;
type of coding jobs that people actually like are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.020
found at startups where they can actually play an&nbsp;
active role in the final product. And people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:12.000
usually more than willing to take a paycut to do&nbsp;
something that they feel is more meaningful. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.160
to minimize this phenomenon, FAANG companies&nbsp;
are usually forced to pay insane salaries.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:21.780
FREE MONEY:&nbsp;

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:26.220
Not only are tech companies trying to attract and&nbsp;
retain developers during a programmer shortage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:32.220
but they also have tons of money to throw around.&nbsp;
This is thanks to the insane margins that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:38.580
unique to the tech industry. Facebook has net&nbsp;
margins between 25 and 30%. Google also has net&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:45.960
margins between 25 and 30%. Meanwhile, Microsoft&nbsp;
has net margins of nearly 40%. With such margins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:51.060
these companies can raise salaries faster than&nbsp;
any other industry. Take fast food for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:56.040
There’s a massive shortage when it comes to&nbsp;
fast food workers as well. But, due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:01.740
limited margins in this industry, salaries can’t&nbsp;
go up all that much. Even Starbucks with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:09.060
$6 coffees only profit 10 to 15%. Not only do&nbsp;
tech companies have far higher margins, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:15.060
also have far fewer employees meaning that their&nbsp;
profit per employee is through the roof. Google’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:23.340
profit per employee is $297,000. Apple’s profit&nbsp;
per employee is $390,000. And Facebook’s profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:29.280
per employee is nearly half a million dollars. The&nbsp;
median salary at Facebook is currently $292,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:35.820
This means that they could literally increase the&nbsp;
pay of all of their employees by $400,000, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:41.490
they’d still have a $100,000 worth of profit left&nbsp;
to spare. In other words, they could pay entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:48.720
level developers $600,000 if they wanted to. If we&nbsp;
do this same math for Starbucks though, it simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.460
doesn’t work out. Before Starbucks drastically&nbsp;
reduced their workforce due to the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:59.940
their employee count stood at 350,000. And on good&nbsp;
years, they profit as much as $6 billion. This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:05.580
however, only works out to a profit per employee&nbsp;
of a little over $17,000 per year. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:10.920
that Starbucks could only increase pay by $9 per&nbsp;
hour before having to charge like $10 per cup of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:16.200
coffee. This is why we don’t hear about Starbuck&nbsp;
baristas being paid six figures due to the fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.820
food shortage, but we do hear about software&nbsp;
engineers being paid seven figures due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:26.460
developer shortage. Also, that’s just half the&nbsp;
story. Something else to note is that the vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:31.500
majority of the high compensations from these tech&nbsp;
companies are stock based compensation. If we take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:37.020
a closer look at Facebook’s 7 figure salary,&nbsp;
we’ll see that only about $400,000 of the $1.6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:44.100
million is actually offered in cash. The other&nbsp;
$1.2 million is offered in stock. Not only is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.180
stock compensation great for employees given that&nbsp;
they’re investing hundreds of thousands if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:52.680
millions into the fastest growing companies in the&nbsp;
world, but it’s also great for companies because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:58.320
it's almost free. Here’s the thing, it’s not like&nbsp;
these companies go onto the stock market and buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:04.020
these shares at market price. Instead, what they&nbsp;
do is dilute existing shareholders to issue new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:09.180
stock to employees. And it turns out that this&nbsp;
is extraordinarily cheap because it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:14.520
come from their bottomline, it comes from their&nbsp;
marketcap. For example, let’s say Facebook wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:19.680
to increase their salaries by $100,000 to attract&nbsp;
more talent. If they increased the salaries of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:26.640
72,000 employees by $100,000, this would cost them&nbsp;
$7.2 billion per year. This is quite an amount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.920
but Facebook could comfortably afford it with&nbsp;
their $40 billion net margin. It would cost them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:36.720
about 18% of their profits. If they offered&nbsp;
the $100,000 in the form of stock, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:43.980
the cost would be relative to their $393 billion&nbsp;
market cap. And $7.2 billion would only cost 1.8%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:50.760
dilution, and that’s with their stock falling off&nbsp;
a cliff. At their peak market cap of $1 trillion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:55.980
this extra compensation would’ve only&nbsp;
cost 0.66% dilution. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:01.740
employees are usually restricted from selling&nbsp;
for a few years, so this dilution wont even hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:08.460
the market for years. This is why companies love&nbsp;
stock compensation. It improves employee loyalty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:13.260
employees make a lot of money, companies can&nbsp;
hand out stupid amounts, and it doesn’t cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:17.880
them much. The one caveat to this though is&nbsp;
that your company has to be growing at a rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:24.240
at which this dilution would be inconsequential.&nbsp;
So, stagnant companies like AT&amp;T or Cisco or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:29.580
Intel can’t leverage this all that much. But,&nbsp;
the FAANG companies, well they go to town.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:34.140
PURSUING CS:&nbsp;

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:38.820
At the end of the day, the reasoning behind&nbsp;
insane software engineering salaries isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:43.260
rocket science. There’s a shortage of talented&nbsp;
software engineers because talented developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:48.180
don’t really like to hang out at big companies&nbsp;
and do menial work. They far prefer to invent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.500
or build something themselves. So, these&nbsp;
companies are having to one up each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:58.200
in terms of pay to retain their developers. And&nbsp;
with insane profit per employee and access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:02.940
easy stock compensation, when these companies&nbsp;
try to outdo each other, salaries basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:07.440
become a game score instead of money. The&nbsp;
funny thing is that these companies still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.000
have an insane amount of headroom. Honestly,&nbsp;
between sacrificing profits and dilution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:18.060
I think Facebook could even afford to pay median&nbsp;
salaries of a million per year. Now, I’m not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:23.700
saying that they should or that they will, but&nbsp;
they could mathematically. So, if you’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:29.760
for a straight forward way to make a stupid amount&nbsp;
of money, CS is definitely the way to go. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:36.060
be warned that the work is not only difficult but&nbsp;
rather monotonous and oftentimes unfulfilling. Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:41.160
you think coders are overpaid? Comment that&nbsp;
down below. Also, drop a like if you wish you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:45.180
were paid this much. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.900
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

